course: dictionary
lesson: 13937
title: Leniently: gentle, not strict
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Leniently is an adverb, so it tells us how someone did something. Its root word is lenient, which means gentle and not strict. Adding -ly turns it into leniently.
explain: «The teacher treated Sam leniently after he forgot his homework.» The clues are forgot and no big trouble. A teacher who is strict would give a bigger punishment, so leniently must mean gently, with a smaller punishment than expected.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The teacher treated Sam strictly after he forgot his homework» sounds like a harsher punishment, so the words change the meaning. Ask yourself: was the person in charge kind and soft, or hard and strict?
fact: Leniently means in a gentle, less strict way.
fact: Leniently often describes how a punishment, a mark or a rule is given: «treated leniently» means the punishment was smaller than expected.
fact: Lenient is the adjective, and leniently is the adverb made from it.
fact: The opposites of leniently are strictly and harshly, which mean with firm, hard rules.
fact: «Leniently» is a good word to use when the person in charge is kind, soft or forgiving.
exercise: «The judge treated the young thief leniently and gave only a small fine.» What does leniently mean? | *In a gentle, less strict way | In a loud and angry way | In a fast and busy way | In a very careful way
exercise: «The referee judged the foul leniently, so the player only got a warning.» What does leniently suggest? | The referee was tired and bored | The referee was very angry and shouted | *The referee was gentle, not strict | The referee was cheating
exercise: Which word means the opposite of leniently? | *Strictly | Happily | Quietly | Lazily
test: «The head teacher dealt with the late pupils leniently.» What does leniently mean here? | Harshly and unfairly | Very quickly, before the bell | *Gently, with a smaller punishment than expected | Loudly across the playground
test: Ben says leniently means very sleepy. What is the right meaning? | Slowly, one step at a time | Very sleepy | Loudly and proudly | *Gently, not strictly
exam: «The coach treated the team leniently after their loss, because they had tried hard.» What does leniently mean? | Cheerfully, with a song | Without any warning at all | *In a gentle, less strict way | Very angrily
